1. PROJECT OVERVIEW
================================================================================

CoffeeChain is a supply chain transparency application for specialty coffee that
adopts the visual and interaction language of the video game LocoRoco. The app
tracks coffee lots from harvest through roasting, displaying provenance data,
cupping scores, and supply chain relationships through a playful, blob-based
interface.

The core premise: serious data presented through joyful, tactile interaction.

Primary Users:
- Coffee roasters tracking green coffee purchases
- Importers managing lot portfolios
- Producers documenting harvest and processing
- Quality control professionals conducting cuppings

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Design Pillars
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Radical simplicity: one piece of information per screen
2. Physicality: every element feels soft, alive, responsive
3. Transparency as texture: data completeness is visible at a glance
4. Black and white: no color, no emoji, clarity through form and motion

================================================================================
2. CORE VISUAL LANGUAGE
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.1 Geometric Foundation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All visual elements reduce to simple geometric primitives:

- Circles: default state, neutral, complete
- Squares: structured data, processed stages, verified information
- Triangles: alerts, flags, incomplete data, attention required

No complex or ornate shapes. Legibility emerges from simplicity. Every form
should be identifiable as a basic shape even when deformed by animation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.2 Animation Principles
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Objects behave like gelatin or soft rubber:

SQUASH AND STRETCH
- Compression on impact or interaction
- Elongation during movement
- Volume preservation: if width increases, height decreases proportionally

DEFORMATION TRIGGERS
- User tap/press: immediate squish response
- Swipe: elements stretch in swipe direction, snap back on release
- Screen transition: elements compress, then expand into new view
- Data loading: gentle pulsing wobble

PHYSICS PARAMETERS (suggested starting values)
- Stiffness: 180-220 (springy but not frantic)
- Damping: 12-18 (settles quickly, no prolonged oscillation)
- Mass: 1.0 (normalized)
- Velocity threshold for squash: 0.5

Animation library recommendation: React Spring or Framer Motion for React
implementations. Both support physics-based spring animations with configurable
stiffness and damping.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.3 Color and Style Constraints
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PALETTE
- Primary: #000000 (black)
- Secondary: #FFFFFF (white)
- Grays for depth: #333333, #666666, #999999, #CCCCCC, #E5E5E5

FORBIDDEN
- Color of any kind (no hue, no saturation)
- Emoji or emoticons
- Iconography that relies on color distinction
- Gradients with color (grayscale gradients permitted)

TEXTURE AND DEPTH
- Extremely subtle noise/grain overlay permitted (2% opacity)
- No drop shadows for layering to preserve 2D effect.
- Border thickness variations to indicate hierarchy. 

TYPOGRAPHY
- Single typeface family recommended
- Suggested: Google Sans or Noto Sans or Bitcount Single.
- Weight variations for hierarchy: 400 (body), 600 (labels), 700 (headings)
- No italics; use weight and size for emphasis

