6. NAVIGATION ARCHITECTURE
================================================================================

Navigation uses swipe gestures in four cardinal directions, with each direction
accessing a distinct layer of information.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.1 Horizontal Axis: Lot Timeline
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The primary view shows a single lot with one feature/stage visible at a time.

SWIPE RIGHT: View Past Stages
- Each swipe reveals the previous stage in the supply chain
- Harvest → Processing → Drying → Milling → Export → Shipping → etc.
- Animation: current view slides right, previous stage slides in from left
- Edge case: at earliest stage (harvest), swipe right bounces back

SWIPE LEFT: View Following Stages
- Each swipe reveals the next stage in the supply chain
- Continues through roasting, retail, etc.
- Past final stage, enters Cupping Suite (see Section 7)

STAGE INDICATOR
- Minimal dots or dashes at screen bottom indicating position in timeline
- Current stage slightly larger or bolder
- No text labels in indicator; stage name appears in view itself

TRANSITION ANIMATION
- Duration: 300ms
- Easing: ease-out for natural deceleration
- Slight scale-down (0.95) of departing view
- Slight scale-up (1.0 from 0.95) of arriving view

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.2 Vertical Axis: Context Layers
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SWIPE UP: Inventory View
- User's tracked/saved lots
- Grid or list of lot blobs (size reflects data richness)
- Profile settings button (top corner)
- Connections to other users (collaborators, trade partners)

Inventory sub-navigation:
- Tap lot to open its timeline view
- Filter controls (by origin, date, visibility score)
- Search functionality

SWIPE DOWN: Global View
- Lot's position within world coffee supply network
- Network visualization showing relationships to other lots
- Deferred implementation (requires database architecture)
- Placeholder: simple radial diagram showing origin region

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.3 Interaction Reduction Principle
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CORE GESTURES (4 total)
- Swipe left: forward in time/stages
- Swipe right: backward in time/stages
- Swipe up: inventory/user layer
- Swipe down: global/network layer

SECONDARY INTERACTIONS
- Tap: select, split aggregated data, trigger squish response
- Hold: merge split data, access contextual options
- Pinch (optional): zoom in global view only

ON-SCREEN BUTTONS (minimal)
- Settings: accessible from inventory view only
- Back/close: for modal or overlay dismissal
- No persistent navigation bar or tab bar

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.4 Screen Structure
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SINGLE FEATURE DISPLAY
Each stage view shows exactly one primary piece of information:

  Example: Processing Stage
  ----------------------------------------
  [                                      ]
  [          PROCESSING                  ]  <- stage label
  [                                      ]
  [            ( O )                     ]  <- lot blob (squishable)
  [                                      ]
  [           Washed                     ]  <- primary data point
  [                                      ]
  [      Dumerso Washing Station         ]  <- secondary context
  [                                      ]
  [        72 hours fermentation         ]  <- tertiary detail
  [                                      ]
  [           · · ● · · ·                ]  <- timeline indicator
  [                                      ]
  ----------------------------------------

INFORMATION HIERARCHY
1. Stage identification (top)
2. Lot blob (center, interactive)
3. Primary data value (large, prominent)
4. Secondary context (medium)
5. Tertiary details (smaller, optional)
6. Timeline position (bottom)

